The script will ask you several questions. Answer Yes (y) to all the license agreements.
Important: When the script asks "Will you specify the Device URI? [Y/n]", type Y.
It will show you a list of connection types. Choose the number corresponding to Specify IP address.
Type in the Static IP address you locked down in Step 1 (e.g., 192.168.68.111).


*** Step 3.2: Add the Printer using the Stable LPD Protocol
Now we add it back manually, pointing directly at the IP address you just locked down.
Still on the http://localhost:631 page, click the Administration tab at the top.
Click the Add Printer button.
Select LPD/LPR Host or Printer and click Continue.
In the Connection box, type exactly this:
lpd://192.168.68.111/BINARY_P1
Click Continue. Give the printer a sensible name (like Brother-DCPJ315W) and click Continue.
Scroll down the "Make" list, select Brother, and click Continue.
Select the Brother DCP-J315W CUPS driver from the list. (If it's not there, you need to run the Brother Linux install script I mentioned in the previous message).
Click Add Printer.


*** Step 4: Verify the Connection Protocol (The "Silver Bullet")
Brother printers communicate best with Linux using the lpd:// protocol, but sometimes installations default to ipp:// or usb://. Let's ensure it's correct.

Open your web browser and go to the hidden Ubuntu printer admin page: http://localhost:631/printers
(If it says "Web Interface is Disabled", open your terminal and run cupsctl WebInterface=yes and refresh the page).

Click on your DCPJ315W printer.

Click the Administration dropdown menu and select Modify Printer. (It may ask for your Ubuntu username and password).

Select LPD/LPR Host or Printer and click Continue.

In the Connection box, type exactly this (replacing the X's with your printer's static IP):
lpd://192.168.X.X/BINARY_P1

Click Continue, select your Brother driver, and click Modify Printer.

Why this works:
By locking the IP address, you stop the printer from moving around the network. By using lpd://[IP_ADDRESS]/BINARY_P1, you are using Brother's native, raw data protocol which bypasses the flaky DNS/mDNS discovery systems that cause Ubuntu to suddenly declare the printer "Offline."



