*** AppArmor
You found the exact root cause in those logs!
Look closely at all these lines:
apparmor="DENIED" operation="create" ... profile="/usr/sbin/cupsd" ... comm="cupsd"
AppArmor (Ubuntu's built-in security shield) is having a meltdown and is actively blocking the CUPS printing service from creating the network sockets it needs to run. Because CUPS is blocked by the security kernel, it just hangs there until systemd finally kills it with a "timeout".
(Note: Your logs show AppArmor is also randomly blocking your system logger rsyslogd, which usually happens after an Ubuntu background update gets stuck).
Here is how to tell AppArmor to back off and let the printer service run.

Step 1: Put the CUPS security profile into "Complain" mode
This tells AppArmor to allow CUPS to do its job, but just log any suspicious activity instead of outright blocking it and crashing the service.

# Install the AppArmor utility tools (if you don't already have them)
sudo apt install apparmor-utils -y

# Tell AppArmor to stop blocking CUPS
sudo aa-complain /usr/sbin/cupsd
